#---
# purpose: "A spec variable shared across two parameters keeps what one position pins when the other observes []"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# Regression target for fz-f98.16. `List.reverse/2` is spec'd
# `([a], [a]) :: [a]`, so `List.reverse(xs, [])` observes `[int]` at the first
# parameter and the empty list at the second. Both bound `a` from below and the
# bounds are joined: `[]`'s element is `none`, the bottom bound, so the second
# adds nothing and `a` is `int`. When it VETOED `a` instead (the fz-f98.16
# cleaner, deleted in fz-kdt.120), `[a]` collapsed to `[]`, the caller's good
# `[int]` argument was narrowed to the empty list, and a second call consuming
# the first's result specialized to the base clause and returned its seed.
#
# The user-defined `My.rev/2` is the same shape written out, so the fixture
# fails whether or not the runtime library happens to spell reverse this way.
defmodule My do
  @spec rev([a], [a]) :: [a]
  fn rev([], tail), do: tail
  fn rev([h | t], acc), do: rev(t, [h | acc])
end

fn main() do
  acc = List.reverse([1, 2, 3], [])
  dbg(acc)
  dbg(List.reverse(acc, []))
  dbg(List.reverse(List.reverse([4, 5, 6], []), []))

  mine = My.rev([1, 2, 3], [])
  dbg(mine)
  dbg(My.rev(mine, []))
end
